<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Both canRequestFilterKeyEvents and the flagRequestFilterKeyEvents flag are
    required; declaring one without the other silently yields no key events at
    all. canRequestFingerprintGestures and flagRequestFingerprintGestures are the
    same pair one subject over, with the same silent failure, which is why they
    are listed together here rather than left to be discovered separately.

    accessibilityEventTypes is deliberately omitted (it defaults to none) and
    canRetrieveWindowContent is false, so this service observes nothing but key
    events and fingerprint swipes: no window titles, no text on screen, no notion
    of which app is in front.

    Fingerprint gestures are the second deliberate widening after canTakeScreenshot
    and a much smaller one: what arrives is a direction, four values wide, and only
    while the sensor is not authenticating — the platform withholds them during an
    unlock, so nothing here can observe or interfere with one. Ordinary unlock is
    untouched. What is worth knowing instead is that most phones deliver these
    gestures at all: only a physical, usually rear-mounted reader reports swipes,
    which is what DeviceCapability.FINGERPRINT_GESTURES exists to say out loud.

    canTakeScreenshot is the one capability beyond that, and it is what
    action.screenshot runs on. It does not imply canRetrieveWindowContent — a
    frame is not a view tree — so that stays false. Android lists every one of
    these capabilities on the enable screen, which is why
    accessibility_service_description names them: a person turning this on for the
    volume-button trigger deserves to read that the same switch lets a macro
    capture the screen and see fingerprint swipes.

    Nothing captures unprompted. takeScreenshot is called from exactly one place
    (ScreenCapture) and only while an action.screenshot node is executing.

    isAccessibilityTool is false because this is an automation feature, not an
    assistive one. Claiming otherwise is what gets apps removed from Play.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagRequestFilterKeyEvents|flagRequestFingerprintGestures"
    android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="true"
    android:canRequestFingerprintGestures="true"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:canTakeScreenshot="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="false"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:summary="@string/accessibility_service_summary" />
